WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:10.400
SIERRA: What does it take to make a hit in
Hollywood? An a-list actress? A tried-and-true

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:23.740
superhero franchise? How about... the blessing
of the People’s Republic of China. For years

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:29.820
now, Hollywood has been straining to make
sure its films don’t offend Chinese censors.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.480
Without their approval, studios can’t gain
access to China’s massive box office. Pleasing

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.730
Chinese censors can mean adding scenes that
glorify the country, but it can also mean

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:46.200
leaving things out, particularly anything
that calls attention to human rights abuses.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.500
The financial pressures to do so have gotten
so intense that in many cases American filmmakers

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.820
are pre-empting censorship altogether, and
designing their films to pass muster from

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:04.409
the first draft. I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and
this is Why It Matters, today, China in the

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:08.650
director’s chair.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:16.950
SIERRA: So, what does China have to do with
Hollywood?

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:22.060
TAGER: So this is a particularly important
year for the China-Hollywood relationship

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:27.000
because this is the year that the Chinese
box office is expected to surpass the American

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.799
box office as the most important box office
in the world.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:36.880
SIERRA: This is James Tager. He is Deputy
Director of Free Expression Research and Policy

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.330
at PEN America, and the author of the PEN
America report, Made in Hollywood, Censored

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.800
by Beijing.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:50.711
TAGER: Now, to get access to that box office,
any movie studio, and this includes Hollywood,

00:01:50.711 --> 00:01:55.820
has to go through a regulatory process set
up by Beijing, set up by the Chinese government.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:01.620
The issue is that this regulatory process
includes censorship, that compliance with

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:07.750
China's censorship model is a prerequisite
to entering the Chinese market. So what we've

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:13.480
seen increasingly is Hollywood studios, firstly,
complying with the censorship and editing

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:18.401
or altering or removing content that Chinese
censors object to. But increasingly, we're

00:02:18.401 --> 00:02:25.010
seeing a sort of more proactive compliance,
an anticipatory compliance and a self censorship,

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:30.401
basically removing things that studios even
suspect will upset the Chinese censor. And

00:02:30.401 --> 00:02:35.250
beyond that sort of working more actively
with Chinese censors and Chinese government

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:40.519
officials to include portrayals of China or
to include portrayals that the Chinese government

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:43.390
particularly likes within their movies.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:48.489
SIERRA: In many cases altered content only
affects the versions that are sent to theaters

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:54.550
in China. But in other cases these changes
are included in the global release too, meaning

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:59.739
that American audiences, and audiences all
over the world, are sometimes watching propaganda

00:02:59.739 --> 00:03:04.660
without even knowing it. It’s shocking when
you think about it for a second, and seems

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:10.250
to undermine a lot of what Hollywood stands
for. So...why are they doing it?

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:17.049
TAGER: Hollywood studios, like any other foreign
studios, in this situation, really have significant

00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:22.940
economic imperatives to cooperate with this
Chinese model of censorship in order to get

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:28.140
into the country. And this is perhaps most
pronounced when we look at Hollywood's biggest

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.000
movies, the blockbusters, the movies that
everyone sees, you know, movies like Iron

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.750
Man 3, where Chinese censors were actually
invited to the set to kind of see what was

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:38.930
going on.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:43.140
Iron Man 3: (Movie Scene)

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:51.049
SIERRA: Released way back in 2013, Iron Man
3 grossed $1.2 billion worldwide. Of that,

00:03:51.049 --> 00:03:57.490
it made $409 million domestically, and $121
million in China. That’s a huge chunk of

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:01.470
change. And the proportion of profits coming
from China continues to grow.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:08.180
TAGER: There is a quota system that is in
place, where the Chinese government will allow

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:13.230
up to 34 movies from outside the country.
Technically, they can bend the rules on this

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:17.910
a little bit, but usually the number is 34.
Many of these spots are obtained by Hollywood

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:23.570
Studios with their biggest blockbusters and
access or lack thereof to the Chinese market

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:28.770
for these big big movies can literally make
or break the profitability of a certain Hollywood

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:29.770
movie.

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:35.720
SIERRA: In 2019, before the pandemic caused
chaos in the film industry, global box office

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:43.919
sales were worth roughly $42.5 billion. Of
this, the U.S. box office accounted for $11.4

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:49.970
billion, and China’s accounted for $9 billion.
While the U.S. was still slightly ahead, its

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:56.291
take had fallen from 2018, whereas China’s
had continued a long trend of growth. The

00:04:56.291 --> 00:05:01.090
pandemic has made it difficult to measure
things accurately, but many think that China’s

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:06.870
market either already has, or will very soon
surpass that of the U.S., and that the trend

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:12.350
will only become more pronounced with time.
And, regardless, China has already become

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:17.210
essential to Hollywood’s bottom line, especially
when it comes to domestic flops. Consider

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:21.470
the 2016 film Warcraft, based on the videogame.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:25.699
Warcraft: (Movie Scene)

00:05:25.699 --> 00:05:32.970
SIERRA: It was reported that just to break
even, the film needed to gross $400 million

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:40.169
world wide. But in the U.S. it got panned
and only brought in $46.7 million. Not to

00:05:40.169 --> 00:05:45.389
fear, China to the rescue! Because of the
popularity of the video game there, it had

00:05:45.389 --> 00:05:50.830
the widest release of any film up to that
point, and pushed profits into the green with

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:55.180
a final total of $422 million.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:06:01.440
TAGER: When it comes to access to the Chinese
market, the gatekeepers are government officials

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:07.410
that have a political agenda, there's a stick
and a carrot, right? The stick is if we don't

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:11.740
like your movie it won't enter. And in fact,
it's even more than that. It's if we don't

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:16.090
like your movie, and we think it actively
sort of insults China or insults the Chinese

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:20.341
government, we may punish your other movies.
We may not let your other movies in, we may

00:06:20.341 --> 00:06:24.540
punish the studio more broadly, we may punish
the people who worked on the project more

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:30.340
broadly. And that really encourages a climate
of self-censorship among Hollywood professionals

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:35.530
who don't want to face political ramifications.
But there's also the carrot. If you sort of

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:42.389
play ball, you may get more favorable release
dates, you may get better terms for advertising

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:47.720
campaigns for the movie within the country,
and you may receive, sort of, the encouragement

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:52.360
of the powers that be that want your movie
to succeed within China. And so between that

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:56.710
carrot and the stick, studios really have
significant incentives to play ball with the

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:58.080
Chinese censor.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:04.050
SIERRA: This carrot and stick tactic works,
even though it is hard to verify with studios.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:08.840
And Hollywood does have a lot to worry about.
The list of movies banned in China is long,

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:13.490
and the reasons for the bans are wide-ranging.
Pirates of the Caribbean: Dead Man's Chest

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:18.370
was banned due to images of ghosts. Call Me
By Your Name was banned for its portrayal

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:24.590
of gay characters. And while it was not stated
explicitly, many suspect that the 2018 film

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:29.750
Christopher Robin was banned because the image
of Winnie the Pooh has been used on Chinese

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:35.020
social media to mock Xi Jinping. The carrot
and stick have worked so well that at this

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:40.150
point Hollywood is dedicating a lot of resources
to keeping its relationship with China humming

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:46.300
along. But it wasn’t always that way. And
you have to wonder, how did it start?

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:56.069
KOKAS: So, film is foundational to the Chinese
nation. 1949, the year of the founding of

00:07:56.069 --> 00:08:01.889
the People's Republic of China, was also the
year of the founding of the Beijing Film Studios,

00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:06.990
which at the very front gate, up until very
recently had statues of a farmer, a worker,

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:13.139
and a soldier, these kind of emblematic parts
of the Chinese nation. Hi, my name is Aynne

00:08:13.139 --> 00:08:17.620
Kokas. And I'm an associate professor of Media
Studies at the University of Virginia. And

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:24.030
I'm a senior faculty fellow at the Miller
Center for Public Affairs. So since the beginning

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:28.702
of China's founding film has been central,
and we've seen all the way through the recent

00:08:28.702 --> 00:08:34.090
years, where films like The Founding of the
Republic, The Founding of the Party, the Founding

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:41.099
of the Army, have been releases, supported
by the party used for the purpose of of celebrating

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:43.399
the growth and the development of the PRC.

00:08:43.399 --> 00:08:49.480
SIERRA: So I know, this is a - a big question.
But what is the timeline of the Hollywood/China

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:51.790
relationship? Can you give us some of the
big milestones?

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:57.209
KOKAS: I think that we can think about a couple
of different key milestones. So there was

00:08:57.209 --> 00:09:02.360
the growth of the fifth generation of filmmakers.
So this was kind of in the 1980s, the first

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:08.300
time when US filmmakers and US studios started
to take notice of Chinese filmmakers. We see

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:13.180
people like Zhang Yimou, coming out of the
fifth generation filmmakers, you guys may

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:17.250
know Zhang Yimou, from, from choreographing
the 2008, Olympic opening and closing ceremonies.

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:24.170
NBC Sports: We’re about to see what happens
when an artist gets nearly unlimited resources.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:28.790
It’s almost a cinematic presentation playing
out in real time.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:33.720
KOKAS: But he started off as a filmmaker who
made kind of art films within the Chinese

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:39.560
studio system in the 80s. Then we fast forward
to 1997, where there's the Hong Kong handover

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:46.089
from the UK to China. And this is a significant
moment in the relationship between China and

00:09:46.089 --> 00:09:50.720
Hollywood, in the sense that we saw previously
a really robust Hong Kong film industry, and

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:54.300
then a shift from Hong Kong to the mainland.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:10:01.480
SIERRA: 1997 is important for a number of
reasons. As the Hong Kong handover took place,

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:06.040
China also began to muscle for control over
how foreign movies portrayed their country

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:07.190
and its government.

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:11.769
TAGER: This was the year when three movies,
all from significant studios, all came out

00:10:11.769 --> 00:10:17.640
that criticize aspects of China's government.
Two of them were on on Tibet, you had “Kundun”

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.360
and “Seven Years in Tibet”, and you had
a movie called “Red Corner” which not

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:25.740
only starred Richard Gere, who was kind of
a Tibet activist and of course, still is,

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:31.560
but already was then, but also criticizing
China's criminal justice system. The Chinese

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:35.340
government responded by saying that not only
would these movies never show in China, but

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:39.800
by saying that the people who worked on them
and the production studios would be punished

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:46.520
for making them, basically putting them on
an economic blacklist. In 1997, the size of

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:51.370
the Chinese box office was kind of the size
of the Peruvian box office, it was not a massive

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:56.079
economic hit that the studios were taking.
But it was when the studios were sort of put

00:10:56.079 --> 00:11:01.720
on notice that the Chinese government would
go beyond just not letting your movie show

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:05.030
in their theaters if they didn't like it,
that they’d take a much more aggressive

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:06.779
retaliatory pose.

00:11:06.779 --> 00:11:12.000
KOKAS: Now, where this really starts to get
going is in 2001, with China's accession into

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.829
the World Trade Organization, because this
creates some requirements for opening up the

00:11:16.829 --> 00:11:21.870
Chinese film industry for other global box
offices. So this gets really interesting in

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:28.959
2012, when the US and China sign a film agreement,
which allows big budget films to enter the

00:11:28.959 --> 00:11:36.210
Chinese market, and there to be a 34 film
floor. Now in 2017, with the Trump administration,

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:43.329
this all expired. And basically, there are
no rules anymore about how any of this happens

00:11:43.329 --> 00:11:48.990
as China's market gets bigger, potentially
than the US market.

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:55.670
SIERRA: What would you say to a studio that
maybe leaned on the idea that they aren't

00:11:55.670 --> 00:12:00.520
accepting Chinese censorship, they're just
trying to be more culturally sensitive and

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:01.520
inclusive.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:06.080
KOKAS: You know, if this is really a question
of representation, show me a growth in the

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:13.579
number of Asian-American directors and producers
and leads in studio films, a growth in women,

00:12:13.579 --> 00:12:19.270
a growth in, people of color, a growth in
LGBTQ folks taking leadership roles, and document

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:21.190
that. And then I can believe what you're saying.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:26.550
TAGER: We know that Hollywood does have a
history of racist stereotypes, lack of representation,

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:32.060
lack of inclusion, and efforts to increase
that representation and inclusion are things

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:39.220
that we applaud. The Chinese censor does not
necessarily want thoughtful three dimensional

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:47.100
depictions of Chinese characters. What they
want is heroic self-aggrandizing depictions

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.350
of Chinese characters and -

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:51.410
SIERRA: Doctors saving Tony Stark type thing?

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:56.120
TAGER: Yeah! That is a great and literal and
specific example that actually happened. To

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:00.880
those who don't know what we’re referring
to, the movie “Iron Man 3”, the company

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:07.130
inserted Chinese-only scenes into the film
that showed in China where all of a sudden,

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:10.810
a series of Chinese doctors save Tony Stark's
life.

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:15.360
Iron Man 3: (Movie Scene)

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:22.160
TAGER: And that's an example of sort of how,
how proactively studios may respond to Chinese

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:28.410
pressure to kind of create more friendly content
in the eyes of the Chinese government.

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:34.190
SIERRA: Can you tell me a little bit about
the Chinese government in general because

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:39.680
I feel like I should probably know that while
analyzing why their involvement in Hollywood

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:40.860
even matters.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:45.810
KOKAS: Something that's important to know
is that regulation of the film industry comes

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:51.990
underneath something called the State Council,
which is China's highest governing body, and

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:59.380
the role of the film industry has moved higher
and higher and higher in Chinese national

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:06.790
oversight over the past 10 years. So we've
seen from previously more local film industry

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:12.519
oversight, to now this State Council oversight.
What this means is that the role of the film

00:14:12.519 --> 00:14:18.259
industry in Chinese political life is increasing
in prominence. It also means that regulations

00:14:18.259 --> 00:14:24.510
are becoming more conservative because individual
municipalities or provinces had a little bit

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:30.070
more flexibility, they could do things kind
of further away from Beijing's eyes. So this

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:35.020
is a really important shift that we're seeing.
Xi Jinping is a known film buff. So this is

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:38.860
something that he he pays attention to kind
of and thinks about personally, it's also

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:44.750
something that has been part of Chinese national
strategies and a wide variety of different

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:45.750
ways.

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:50.100
SIERRA: Longtime Why It Matters listeners
will know that Xi Jinping has emerged as the

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:55.250
most powerful and assertive Chinese leader
since Mao Zedong. The special interest he

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:59.791
has taken in film is just a small part of
a wider strategy of asserting China’s power

00:14:59.791 --> 00:15:05.940
on the world stage while using economic, diplomatic
and cultural leavers to try to change global

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:12.440
perceptions of his authoritarian regime. Xi’s
focus on censorship goes far beyond film,

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:17.600
including the “great firewall” that bars
Chinese citizens from accessing western platforms

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:20.040
like Google and Facebook.

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:26.540
SIERRA: So what is the Chinese model of censorship?
I mean, I know you're saying that there's

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:33.079
no specific list, but in general, you know,
what kind of themes and scenes get cut? And

00:15:33.079 --> 00:15:35.720
on the same token, like, you know, what kind
of scenes do they like?

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:42.060
TAGER: Well, they like scenes that portray
China and its governance in a positive light,

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:48.250
as a leader, being sort of strong and impressive.
A great example that I would point to is 2014,

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:55.329
“Transformers: Age of Extinction,” which
began as a joint production with a Chinese

00:15:55.329 --> 00:15:58.990
state owned company, The China Movie Channel.

00:15:58.990 --> 00:16:06.310
Transformers: Age of Extinction: (Movie Scene)

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:12.610
KOKAS: One of the things that's really interesting
about the film is that in order to save Hong

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:16.290
Kong from from the Transformers, they have
to make a call to the Chinese minister of

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:23.960
defense in Beijing, who then sends naval ships
into Victoria Harbour in Hong Kong as a way

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:29.260
to assert China's protection of its national
security, vis a vis Hong Kong. And it's these

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:35.730
same arguments that are being used to do things
like arrest activists Joshua Wong and Agnes

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:42.191
Chow. So we see a Michael Bay film echoing
Chinese political discourse about its relationship

00:16:42.191 --> 00:16:44.480
with Hong Kong, which is really strange.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:50.220
TAGER: Now, a lot of observers have pointed
out that the Chinese officials in that movie

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:54.880
are really seen as selfless and benevolent,
particularly in their willingness to defend

00:16:54.880 --> 00:17:00.610
Hong Kong from this alien threat. Now, the
film was released the same year as the Umbrella

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:05.390
Movement protests in Hong Kong calling for
greater democratic freedoms. So we can really

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:11.600
see the political interest of the Chinese
government in being portrayed as a benevolent,

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:17.640
paternalistic defender of Hong Kong from these
alien forces, at the very time that there

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:20.200
was a massive pro democracy movement.

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:25.640
SIERRA: Unlike most Chinese citizens, residents
of Hong Kong enjoyed democratic freedoms and

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:32.140
civil liberties for decades, even after Britain
handed the territory over to China in 1997.

00:17:32.140 --> 00:17:36.770
But in recent years, a series of crackdowns
have made it seem like the days of Hong Kong’s

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:42.470
freedom are numbered. This gives an entirely
different context to a scene portraying the

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:48.020
Chinese navy entering Hong Kong’s harbor
in order to protect it. And to Hong Kong citizens

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:52.659
who are fighting to preserve their freedom,
something far more menacing than portrayals

00:17:52.659 --> 00:17:54.679
of heroic Chinese doctors.

00:17:54.679 --> 00:18:01.040
KOKAS: So the fact that this film was released
the weekend before July 1, which is the anniversary

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:06.289
of Hong Kong returning to the PRC, was really
a significant period of time.

00:18:06.289 --> 00:18:10.230
SIERRA: Yeah, I mean, that's pretty wild,
because here in the US, most of us would have

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:16.060
just seen, you know, a heroic moment, we wouldn't
have noticed any of these undertones.

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:21.720
TAGER: But one thing I do want to note is,
I think a lot of people just assume it's sort

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:26.230
of the regular political messages that are
seen as out of bounds. You know, don't mention

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:30.130
Tibet, don't mention Taiwan, don't mention
the Dalai Lama, don't talk about the Umbrella

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:34.970
Protests, but the censorship system for movies
is really a lot broader than that. It really

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:40.480
is dynamic and censors have wide latitude
to push for the changes they want. And many

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:46.500
of the things that are blocked are tropes,
or genres, that, I think if you're not Chinese,

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:50.920
you'd be very surprised to hear about because
they just wouldn't seem political and they

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:56.710
wouldn't seem problematic. China's long standing
ban on ghost stories is a great example. There

00:18:56.710 --> 00:19:00.330
are several different reasons that are given
for this, you know, the most common one is

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:05.900
this idea of kind of getting rid of superstition
and discouraging superstition among the populace.

00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:09.919
However, in researching this is, there's one
body of thought that says, well, actually,

00:19:09.919 --> 00:19:15.309
ghost stories are really much more inherently
political in China than they are in many other

00:19:15.309 --> 00:19:20.700
countries because they were traditionally
a vehicle by which you could criticize dead

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:26.820
corrupt officials by portraying them as ghosts.
Time travel is another one. Time travel stories

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:32.950
are really kind thema non grata, to mix and
match my latin, within China and this will

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:38.610
affect the movies that get to be shown. “Looper”
is an interesting example. “Looper” was

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:44.659
a joint production and some people may recall
the movie, Bruce Willis, Joseph Gordon Levitt,

00:19:44.659 --> 00:19:50.270
time travel was a big part of the movie. Now,
it started as the script, the movie was set

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:57.280
between Kansas and Paris. When Chinese financing
and Chinese co-production came in, they proposed

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:02.500
let's make this a joint production and let's
instead of Paris, let's film it in China.

00:20:02.500 --> 00:20:08.210
And so one of the explanations that one of
the producers gave was, we knew it would be

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:13.200
hard to make a time travel movie and get it
into China so we figured, okay, if we make

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:18.169
the time travel movie about China in the future
and it makes China look very good, we have

00:20:18.169 --> 00:20:22.990
a much higher chance of getting it sort of
approved to be shown within China. So this

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:27.400
is an example of the way that sort of business
and the political interests of the Chinese

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:32.341
government kind of coincide and I think there
are many business people who say, well, you

00:20:32.341 --> 00:20:37.970
always look for the win-win. The issue here
is one of the people winning in these situations

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:43.870
is a government that is trying to push a specific
political narrative through film.

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:48.570
SIERRA: So you mentioned joint productions
with the Chinese government. Can you give

00:20:48.570 --> 00:20:51.429
me some details on how these partnerships
work?

00:20:51.429 --> 00:20:56.330
TAGER: So we talk about joint productions
as, almost like a tripartite relationship:

00:20:56.330 --> 00:21:00.789
you have the foreign film making group, the
domestic filmmaking group, and then the censor

00:21:00.789 --> 00:21:06.540
as sort of this invisible third partner. What
this does is really allow the Chinese government

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:12.730
a lot of leeway to influence the structure,
story, and themes of the film. And it's a

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:18.950
model that's growing as we speak. Particularly
as Chinese production companies become larger,

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:23.210
more technologically sophisticated with what
they can pull off, more significant players

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:24.429
in this field.

00:21:24.429 --> 00:21:30.820
KOKAS: So this is a really interesting shift
in the dynamic. There's something called co-production,

00:21:30.820 --> 00:21:35.970
which is essentially when Hollywood studios
go to China and try to make a film there.

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:40.650
And as part of that condition, they have to
give Chinese regulators oversight into the

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:45.910
production process. So everything from pre-production,
like, what's the idea to post production,

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:50.960
so final cut approval. And then there's something
that I talk about in my book “Hollywood:

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:55.159
Made in China” called a faux-production,
where companies try to do this, they try to

00:21:55.159 --> 00:21:58.140
go through the process, but frankly, it's
a huge headache, having a having a Chinese

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:02.270
regulator, you know, check up on your on your
film production is not something that most,

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:06.669
you know, freewheeling Hollywood executives
would be very excited about. So sometimes

00:22:06.669 --> 00:22:10.460
they pull back. But the part that's really
interesting is, even after pulling back, we

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:13.820
still see examples of potential Chinese government
influence.

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:20.289
SIERRA: Right. And as a viewer, you may not
even notice or realize that it's important

00:22:20.289 --> 00:22:24.871
in some way? But you're saying it's setting
a standard and planting a seed in the people

00:22:24.871 --> 00:22:25.980
who are viewing it.

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:29.590
KOKAS: Right, exactly. And I would argue,
actually, this is it's much worse when it

00:22:29.590 --> 00:22:34.520
happens in a film like Transformers 4, than
if it happens in some kind of historical drama

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:40.800
where people are primed to think about questions
of politics and history and human rights.

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:44.330
In Transformers 4 people just want to see
the explosions, and this is kind of slipping

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:45.490
in underneath.

00:22:45.490 --> 00:22:51.659
SIERRA: But there’s another recent example
that has a bit of a different story, and that’s

00:22:51.659 --> 00:22:52.789
“Mulan”.

00:22:52.789 --> 00:22:56.190
Mulan: (Movie Scene)

00:22:56.190 --> 00:23:02.419
SIERRA: So I feel like part of this conversation
has to be me asking about “Mulan,” because

00:23:02.419 --> 00:23:08.309
I remember reading a lot about “Mulan”
and some of the controversy around it. What

00:23:08.309 --> 00:23:10.120
role does that movie play in all this?

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:16.059
TAGER: Yeah, so there's more than one controversy
around “Mulan,” unfortunately. And when

00:23:16.059 --> 00:23:21.840
we did our report, we talked about the fact
that the star of “Mulan,” Chinese-American

00:23:21.840 --> 00:23:27.230
actress Crystal Liu, she made several comments
being supportive of the Hong Kong police during

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:32.270
a wave of Hong Kong pro democracy protests.
And one of the interesting things about that

00:23:32.270 --> 00:23:38.970
was how there appeared to be a government-backed
social media campaign against the Hong Kong

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:45.120
protests under the hashtag #SupportMulan.
So it was literally a government-backed messaging

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:51.020
narrative campaign using Mulan and Crystal
Liu’s comment to push a political message.

00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:56.350
KOKAS: It was so shocking to see that the
Chinese that the film itself that “Mulan”

00:23:56.350 --> 00:24:01.669
was actually echoing a lot of Chinese government
discourse, a lot of Chinese government language

00:24:01.669 --> 00:24:08.799
about how it's necessary to quash Western
rebellions as they were filming in Xinjiang,

00:24:08.799 --> 00:24:14.800
this site that the Chinese government deems
its site of Western rebellion. So there's

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:20.690
a whole history of the Chinese government
having difficulties with minorities in the

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:24.559
west of China. So we think about Tibet, we
think about Xinjiang, because these were places

00:24:24.559 --> 00:24:29.970
that had, you know, long histories and traditions
separate from the Han Chinese tradition. And

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:36.980
in “Mulan” what we saw was this valorization
of Chinese imperial culture, and the treatment

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:43.220
of minorities in the west of China as barbarians,
who needed to be quashed, and Disney made

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:49.820
that film. And they made it in sites where
there are concentration camps for Uighurs.

00:24:49.820 --> 00:24:55.340
SIERRA: For those who don’t know, the situation
in China's northwestern Xinjiang province

00:24:55.340 --> 00:25:00.720
is often cited as the most shocking sign of
China’s growing authoritarianism under Xi

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:06.000
Jinping. The region is the site of detention
camps where more than a million members of

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:12.789
the Muslim Uyghur minority are subjected to
population control, forced labor, torture,

00:25:12.789 --> 00:25:19.030
sexual abuse, family separation and repression
of cultural and religious expression. The

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:23.600
Chinese government, for its part, has denied
that the abuses are taking place, despite

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:28.549
the fact that they have been documented by
experts, journalists, survivors and government

00:25:28.549 --> 00:25:29.750
officials.

00:25:29.750 --> 00:25:35.649
TAGER: At the credits of the movie, they literally
thank several government agencies that are

00:25:35.649 --> 00:25:41.690
either actively involved in or propagandizing
against the human rights abuses that are going

00:25:41.690 --> 00:25:47.399
on as we speak, in Xinjiang -- again, the
internment of over a million Muslims there.

00:25:47.399 --> 00:25:54.440
And the revelation that the film has scenes
that were shot presumably very close to where

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:59.190
these internment centers are, and that the
movie sort of went out of its way to thank

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:04.440
these people who are involved in human rights
abuses, is genuinely deeply troubling.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:10.559
KOKAS: There were boycotts related to this
issue as well as the Hong Kong issue and the

00:26:10.559 --> 00:26:16.680
only time that Disney kind of spoke publicly
about this in the period immediately following

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:22.000
its release was a discussion by the CFO who
was talking about, you know, issues with the

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:24.820
film, presumably related to its financial
performance.

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:29.659
SIERRA: So nothing about the human rights
abuses happening right next to their film

00:26:29.659 --> 00:26:30.659
sites?

00:26:30.659 --> 00:26:36.760
KOKAS: Not explicitly. It should be noted
that in an official response, Disney insisted

00:26:36.760 --> 00:26:41.929
that it filmed in Xinjiang for “authenticity”,
and that their inclusion of thanks to Chinese

00:26:41.929 --> 00:26:46.690
authorities in the credits was in keeping
with standard practices. Listeners can find

00:26:46.690 --> 00:26:48.809
their full response in the shownotes.

00:26:48.809 --> 00:26:54.940
TAGER: I think that is emblematic of the larger
attitude that Hollywood as an industry has

00:26:54.940 --> 00:26:59.160
taken towards this issue, which is, you know,
they don't see much they don't see any upside

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:04.110
to them dealing with it, they're under significant
economic imperatives to play ball with the

00:27:04.110 --> 00:27:10.179
Chinese government, and they see engaging
in this issue further as a lose-lose proposition,

00:27:10.179 --> 00:27:14.250
and they'd like everyone to just kind of move
on and they hope that the issue sort of goes

00:27:14.250 --> 00:27:28.399
away. In fact, we expect that it will become
worse for studios that the pressures on them

00:27:28.399 --> 00:27:33.840
to comply with these censorship strictures
will become more and more intense, given how

00:27:33.840 --> 00:27:40.179
the Chinese box office is simply becoming
more and more important to studios, and conversely,

00:27:40.179 --> 00:27:45.440
that the domestic filmmakers and filmmaking
students in China are becoming more and more

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:51.799
able to pull off big blockbuster movies. So
Chinese film-going audiences need Hollywood

00:27:51.799 --> 00:27:57.600
less than less to get their fix of a blockbuster
spectacle. But Hollywood as an industry needs

00:27:57.600 --> 00:28:00.850
access to Chinese audiences more and more.

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:08.279
KOKAS: So I think that this demonstrates the
challenge that the US faces over the next

00:28:08.279 --> 00:28:14.210
20 to 30 years. The US system is very focused
on global profitability and we have a lot

00:28:14.210 --> 00:28:20.179
of companies that are, you know, listed on
our stock exchanges that need to continue

00:28:20.179 --> 00:28:25.140
growing and the way that they continue growing
is through expanding global markets. China

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:29.809
is a really important global market. So it's,
in some ways an existential question about

00:28:29.809 --> 00:28:34.580
the US system. To what degree do we continue
prioritizing economic growth and economic

00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:39.039
growth in China? and to what degree do we
take into account these other issues?

00:28:39.039 --> 00:28:44.970
SIERRA: What's the price we pay as a society
for allowing a repressive regime to sorta

00:28:44.970 --> 00:28:48.799
shape the types of media we produce, and even
the stories we tell?

00:28:48.799 --> 00:28:55.380
TAGER: The cost will never be fully visible,
and that's exactly the problem, right? The

00:28:55.380 --> 00:29:00.820
largest cost of this will be the movies that
never get made, or that never gets written

00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:07.049
or filmed in the first place. This space for
storytelling will narrow. And Hollywood is

00:29:07.049 --> 00:29:13.570
the world's largest storytelling center. And
where Hollywood goes on this, so go other

00:29:13.570 --> 00:29:19.830
storytelling centers. I mean, Hollywood is
not the only film industry that is being affected

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:27.500
by this. So other film centers in the world
Europe, India, Nigeria, as just examples,

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:32.909
they having far less resources and access
to, say, cutting edge technology than Hollywood

00:29:32.909 --> 00:29:37.669
does, if Hollywood is not going to stand up
to this influence, it's hard for us to expect

00:29:37.669 --> 00:29:43.780
other smaller film centers to do so. So what
we're talking about is a global shaping of

00:29:43.780 --> 00:29:48.299
the perhaps the most important storytelling
medium that's ever existed to date, which

00:29:48.299 --> 00:29:54.820
is movies. And the movies that people consume
influence their perception of what's going

00:29:54.820 --> 00:30:00.910
on in the world. So the idea that those movies
by and large would be influenced systemically

00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:06.210
by an autocratic government is something that
I'm not overstating when I say could have

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:07.330
global consequences.

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:14.019
SIERRA: So would the US government ever step
in something like this? Should they?

00:30:14.019 --> 00:30:17.460
KOKAS: I mean, I think that there's not a
there's not a big place for it. And let's

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:20.720
be honest, like anytime the US government
has gotten involved in the film industry,

00:30:20.720 --> 00:30:25.059
and in the past of the US, it hasn't really
turned out that well, we don't want, like,

00:30:25.059 --> 00:30:29.960
a new McCarthy era. I think that there are
a couple of things that can be done in terms

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:36.240
of just like tracing financial transactions,
requiring more reporting about who's funding

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:41.720
what and that's something that I think studios
can do and the US government can require that

00:30:41.720 --> 00:30:47.529
doesn't necessarily lead to the senators deciding
what types of content gets produced in Hollywood,

00:30:47.529 --> 00:30:48.529
because that's a lose-lose.

00:30:48.529 --> 00:30:56.050
TAGER: You know, as a matter of general principle,
we're very leery of the idea of having governmental

00:30:56.050 --> 00:31:03.019
intervention to save free speech from governmental
intervention, right? This is an area where

00:31:03.019 --> 00:31:08.889
anyone who's looking into a potential governmental
or legislative solution on the US side should

00:31:08.889 --> 00:31:15.210
step pretty gingerly. And it really underscores
the fact that what's best is if the industry

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:21.549
on its own, takes unified and affirmative
action on this. Both to protect their own

00:31:21.549 --> 00:31:26.390
interests, and because it just is objectively
a better idea in terms of free speech and

00:31:26.390 --> 00:31:32.120
artistic expression to act before government
figures, either rightly or wrongly feel like

00:31:32.120 --> 00:31:37.750
they need to act instead. Right? That's why
one of our recommendations, is has been a

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:43.309
voluntary disclosure mechanism that we think
the industry should take on in a unified at

00:31:43.309 --> 00:31:49.440
large way, were somewhat similar to what Silicon
Valley does, where they kind of periodically

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:55.870
post their takedown requests they've received
from governments around the world and we think

00:31:55.870 --> 00:32:00.950
that there's a model there that could be followed
from Hollywood as an industry being like,

00:32:00.950 --> 00:32:04.230
these are the censorship requests we've received
from around the world.

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:09.730
SIERRA: Do you think that more disclosure
of how these decisions are made would help

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:13.611
this whole process along and sort of bring
it to light and then, you know, we could we

00:32:13.611 --> 00:32:15.909
could clean it up and get it taken care of?

00:32:15.909 --> 00:32:19.970
KOKAS: I mean, I think that's really difficult,
right? Because a lot of these are creative

00:32:19.970 --> 00:32:25.850
processes. So it's just as easy to say that
this is just the kind of creative vision of

00:32:25.850 --> 00:32:29.620
these directors. And that's not something
that I think we want to start trampling on

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:34.779
within the US context, which is why I think
following the money is a way that I feel more

00:32:34.779 --> 00:32:40.500
comfortable with. So, you know, maybe we set
limits on the amount of funding that can come

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:46.049
from certain firms. Or maybe we set limits
on certain firms that can invest in Hollywood

00:32:46.049 --> 00:32:50.840
studio productions. That would be kind of
as far as I would feel comfortable going.

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:55.940
And I think that we also need to have conversations
like this so that consumers have an understanding

00:32:55.940 --> 00:33:01.159
of how these market systems operate. And maybe
push back on those more broadly.

00:33:01.159 --> 00:33:05.380
TAGER: One of the things we talk about in
our report as a recommendation is we would

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:11.220
love to see American studios and filmmaking
communities do more to encourage and partner

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:17.780
with independent Chinese filmmakers, who often
are underground or exiled if they don't want

00:33:17.780 --> 00:33:23.389
to participate in the censorship regime. We
applaud cultural interchange and we always

00:33:23.389 --> 00:33:28.271
want to see more of it. And we don't want
anyone to listen to, to this recording, or

00:33:28.271 --> 00:33:33.139
to read our report and think, oh, the solution
is like, let's spend less time talking about

00:33:33.139 --> 00:33:35.220
Chinese themes or let’s just -

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:37.990
SIERRA: Don't do anything with China ever
again, right?

00:33:37.990 --> 00:33:44.580
TAGER: We want cultural interchange, the problem
occurs when the sole gatekeepers to that cultural

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:52.450
interchange use it as an opportunity to apply
politically motivated censorship. So we encourage

00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:57.809
anyone who works in the film world to do what
they can to work with Chinese filmmakers and

00:33:57.809 --> 00:34:02.919
tell Chinese stories, but we encourage them
to do so in whatever way they can to avoid

00:34:02.919 --> 00:34:08.030
or to push back against this type of censorship.
At the end of the day, we believe that sunlight

00:34:08.030 --> 00:34:13.480
remains the best disinfectant. Hollywood enjoys
a reputation of being willing to speak truth

00:34:13.480 --> 00:34:18.450
to power here at home and by home, I refer
to the United States. To the point where you

00:34:18.450 --> 00:34:23.530
have some Americans who are like, my goodness,
these unpatriotic Hollywood figures, right?

00:34:23.530 --> 00:34:28.389
But they have that reputation of being willing
to speak truth to power. We think that Hollywood

00:34:28.389 --> 00:34:34.310
as an industry has been less vocal on this
issue when it comes to censorship in the Chinese

00:34:34.310 --> 00:34:39.060
government than it has been in making sure
to push back against any attempted censorship

00:34:39.060 --> 00:34:44.149
from the United States government. We're asking
for the same standard that Hollywood applies

00:34:44.149 --> 00:34:48.829
as an industry to its own relationship with
governmental figures here in the United States

00:34:48.829 --> 00:34:53.320
in protecting artistic freedom. We're asking
them to apply that same standard to its relationships

00:34:53.320 --> 00:34:56.360
with other governments around the world.

00:34:56.360 --> 00:35:04.589
SIERRA: China’s box office is only going
to grow, and it may seem impossible to dissuade

00:35:04.589 --> 00:35:10.380
Hollywood from a profit motive that strong.
But still, in recent years the American public

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:15.910
has demonstrated its power to force Hollywood
to make incremental progress on other issues.

00:35:15.910 --> 00:35:20.980
Moviegoers have been increasingly vocal about
representations of gender and race, and Hollywood

00:35:20.980 --> 00:35:27.170
has begun to realize that ignoring these concerns
can come at a cost. It would be good, perhaps,

00:35:27.170 --> 00:35:31.849
if “appeasing authoritarian governments
and human rights abusers” was added to that

00:35:31.849 --> 00:35:37.040
list. But to make this happen audiences need
to become more literate about what’s going

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:42.260
on in the rest of the world. Understanding
the situation in Hong Kong, or in Xinjiang,

00:35:42.260 --> 00:35:47.780
can help you read between the lines and see
the true cost of censorship. The United States

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:52.609
still has a lot of soft power, and Hollywood
is one of the best examples of how that power

00:35:52.609 --> 00:35:58.369
touches the world. We would never accept censorship
from our own government, why should we accept

00:35:58.369 --> 00:36:13.839
it from any other? For resources used in this
episode and more information, visit CFR.org/Whyitmatters

00:36:13.839 --> 00:36:18.120
and take a look at the show notes. Have a
question or some feedback? Feel like saying

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:25.840
hey? Send us an email at whyitmatters@cfr.org.
